  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=11",
      "html": "<p><strong>Scientific name:</strong> Clarias angolensis Steindachner, 1866</p><p><strong>Common name:</strong> Angolan Walking Catfish</p><p><strong>Type locality:</strong> Angola.</p><p><strong>Size:</strong> 35 cm SL (13.8\")</p><p><strong>Etymology:</strong> Clarias: from the Greek chlaros, meaning lively, in reference to the ability of the fish to live for long periods out of water. Named after Angola, its country of discovery.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=11",
      "html": "<p>Africa: Lower and Central Congo system. Trade restricted in Germany (Anl.3 BArtSchV).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=11",
      "html": "<ul><li>pH: 6.8 - 8.5</li><li>Temperature: 22.0-25.0°C</li><li>Able to survive in most water conditions.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "equipment",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=11",
      "html": "<p>Open spaces for swimming and some cover is required.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=11",
      "html": "<p>Omnivorous and predatory. Will eat virtually anything that can be considered edible.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=11",
      "html": "<p>Must be housed with lively, robust large fish. Suggested tankmates: large cyprinids (silver sharks, tinfoil or spanner barbs for example) or Central American cichlids.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=11",
      "html": "<p>Males are brighter and more slender than females.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=11",
      "html": "<p>Sexually mature at 2.5 years. Spawning in pairs. Lithophilous species. Fecundity over 500 cream-coloured eggs (diameter of around 2 mm). The male protects the clutch. Incubation period 1.5 to 2 days, after 4-5 days the young start feeding. There is no breeding report.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=11",
      "html": "<p>Clarias species are characterized by an elongated body; a soft rayed dorsal fin extending to, or nearly to, the caudal fin base; a soft rayed anal fin extending from just behind the anus to the caudal fin base; pectoral fins each with a serrated anterior bony spine; head depressed, covered largely by firmly sutured, surface sculptured bony plates forming a protective helmet; four pairs of flagellate barbels (nasals, maxillaries, inner and outer mandibulars); air breathing organs derived from the 2nd and 4th epibranchials within a superbranchial chamber.</p><p>Coloration is brownish, albino varieties are also known.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=11",
      "html": "<p>Verhandlungen der K.-K. zoologisch-botanischen Gesellschaft in Wien v. 16, pp 766, Pl. 13 (figs. 4-7).</p>"
    }
  ],
